Serial Peripheral Interface SPI Protocol
========================================

* SPI protocol was designed by Motorola before I2C on board communication for short distance communication (within 9 ft range)

		If master and slave are on the same board it is called as on board 

* SPI designed for mutli slave communication

		
Real Time Applications / Projects of using SPI
==============================================

	1. Mobile phone's
	2. Laptops / Desktops
	3. Smartwatches 
	4. Infotainment system of the CAR 
	5. Cameras
	6. MP3 players

	
List of SPI slaves
==================

	1. External ADC's
	2. External EEPROM's
	3. Micro SD cards (not present in I2C protocol)
	4. External CAN controllers
	5. Sensors
	6. Touch screen displays


	In the SPI hardware setup master is mandatory 
	
	Master only can start the communication not the slave 

	

Features of SPI Protocol 
========================

	1. SPI was designed by Motorola in the mid 1980s

	2. SPI is 4 wired communication protocol 

		1. Master in, slave out [send/write]
		2. Master out, slave in [receive/read]
		3. Serial clock (CLK/SCLK)
		4. Chip select / slave select (CS/SS)
	
		 To communicate with a slave, the master deselects all other slaves, selects the target slave (CS = 0), performs read/write, and then deselects it (CS = 1)

	
	3. SPI is full duplex, synchronous, serial communication protocol 

		In SPI full duplex communication occurs in every clock pulse. which means when one clock pulse is generated master will send 1 bit information to slave and master receives 1 bit information from slave. 

	4. SPI is multi slave communication protocol 

		in I2C protocol we cannot connect ADC, ADC, ADC its like having same ID.
		but in I2C we can 

	5. Maximum no.of slaves that can be connected on the SPI bus, are restricted by the hardware

		Number of SPI slaves = Number of CS pins provided by master

	6. Speed of communication 

		SPI does not define any speed limit; implementations often reach over 10 Mbps

	7. Acknowledgement is not supported by the SPI 

	8. Direction of data transmission can be MSB first or LSB first 

		In I2C => [1 byte] Ack [1 byte] Ack [1 byte]	// MASTER will know data has been reached
		
		In SPI => [1 byte] [1 byte] [1 byte]	// MASTER won't know if the data have been received 

		In SPI protocol the programming changes from slave to slave 
	
	9. SPI has 4 modes of operations and modes are dependent on the 2 parameters

		1. clock phase (CPHA)
		2. clock polarity (CPOL)

		* Clock phase represents at which edge (rising/falling) data reading (sampling) takes place 
		
		* Clock polarity represents base value of the clock (starting value)

		
		
